WEBVTT

00:00:00.103 --> 00:00:03.603
This runnable example demonstrates a business-rule
constraint

00:00:05.439 --> 00:00:07.636
First run the completed version with a

00:00:07.662 --> 00:00:11.823
valid appointment time PostgreSQL should accept it

00:00:13.679 --> 00:00:15.429
Then change the ending time so that

00:00:15.442 --> 00:00:17.179
it is earlier than the starting time

00:00:17.192 --> 00:00:20.968
and run the example again The SQL

00:00:20.994 --> 00:00:23.494
syntax is still valid but the requested

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:25.823
row now violates the business rule enforced

00:00:25.849 --> 00:00:29.750
by the CHECK constraint That difference is

00:00:29.776 --> 00:00:33.581
important Syntax answers whether the statement is

00:00:33.594 --> 00:00:37.528
written correctly Constraints answer whether the
requested

00:00:37.555 --> 00:00:41.416
data is allowed Run both versions in

00:00:41.429 --> 00:00:43.363
the browser and read the error message

00:00:43.390 --> 00:00:47.449
on the invalid one In pgAdmin you

00:00:47.462 --> 00:00:49.778
would see the same idea a database-level

00:00:49.804 --> 00:00:52.331
constraint rejects bad data regardless of which

00:00:52.357 --> 00:00:53.725
application submitted it
